qesR copyright and third-party content
======================================

The MIT licence of qesR (the LICENSE file; "License: MIT + file LICENSE" in
DESCRIPTION) covers the qesR code only, Copyright (c) 2026 Thomas
Gareau-Paquette: its R code, tests and build scripts, and what was written
for qesR itself (its documentation and harmonization rules). It does not
cover the data and metadata that come from the studies qesR reads or from
other sources: each keeps its own licence, given below. This file lists
that content, where it is in the package, where it comes from and under
which licence:

  1. metadata of the studies released under CC0 1.0 (public domain);
  2. metadata of the 2022 Quebec Election Study, licensed CC BY-NC 4.0
     (attribution, non-commercial), NOT covered by the MIT licence;
  3. other sources: Élections Québec results (not in the package) and
     Statistics Canada census counts (Statistics Canada Open Licence).

No survey data ship with qesR. The package contains no respondent rows from
any study: get_qes() downloads each data file from its Dataverse deposit when
you ask for it. The demonstration study "qes_demo" (inst/extdata/demo/) is
synthetic: 60 respondents drawn at random by data-raw/make_demo.R. Its
variable names and labels are a subset of those of the Quebec Election Study
2014 SPSS file (CC0 1.0, doi:10.5683/SP3/64F7WR); no value comes from a real
respondent.


1. Study metadata released under CC0 1.0
----------------------------------------

For the studies below, released by their authors under the Creative Commons
CC0 1.0 Universal public domain dedication
(https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/), qesR ships metadata
taken from the deposited data files and documents:

  - inst/extdata/dict/variables.csv.gz: variable names and labels from the
    pinned data file, and question text in English and French from the
    deposited questionnaires and codebooks (column doc_ref names the
    document and question of each text);
  - inst/extdata/dict/values.csv.gz: value labels from the data file (for
    qes2018, from its French questionnaire) and the unweighted count of each
    code;
  - inst/extdata/catalog/: deposit titles, author lists, dataset versions,
    file names, sizes and checksums, as published on each deposit, and short
    descriptions of each study that quote its codebook;
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/: the harmonization spec written for qesR (its
    rules are qesR's, MIT; its qes2022 content is section 2), which quotes
    the question text of the items it maps (the same text as the
    dictionary), their value labels, and unweighted counts from the data
    files: in the text of its rows, in gates.csv (how many respondents of a
    wave gave each answer to a question and to the question that filters
    it) and in
    expected/marginals.csv (how many fall in each harmonized category);
    expected/hashes.csv holds md5 hashes of the harmonized columns. The
    relaxed layer of qes_decon() quotes them the same way: relaxed_maps.csv
    (question text, and counts in its evidence),
    expected/relaxed_marginals.csv (how many fall in each relaxed category)
    and expected/relaxed_hashes.csv (md5 hashes of the relaxed columns).

CC0 requires no attribution. qesR names the source of each study anyway, as
the authors ask users to cite the data (qes_cite() gives the full citation):

