All these deposits are on Borealis (https://borealisdata.ca).

The three 1998 surveys cover francophones only. The codebook of the deposit
says it was decided to keep only francophones ("retenir uniquement les
francophones"), because CREATEC had surveyed only the francophone
population. The CREATEC file holds respondents whose mother tongue is
French (question Q17: all 1,057); the CROP file interviewed 450
respondents in French and kept, for its post-election wave, the 426 whose
language spoken most often in the household is French (question q22). The
combined qes1998 panel file pools these 1,057 CREATEC and 426 CROP
respondents: its rows link one to one to the firms' files (quetr; for CROP,
quest = quetr - 60000), which confirms the definition firm by firm.


2. The 2022 study: metadata licensed CC BY-NC 4.0, not MIT
-----------------------------------------------------------

  qes2022             2022 Quebec Election Study. Mahéo, Bélanger,        10.7910/DVN/PAQBDR
                      Stephenson, Harell (Harvard Dataverse)

The 2022 Quebec Election Study is licensed by its authors under the
Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International licence (CC
BY-NC 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/; legal code:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/legalcode). qesR ships
metadata derived from its data file (file 7449513) and its codebook (file
7449514). The parts of the files below that concern qes2022 (their rows
whose study is qes2022, or whose map_id names qes2022) are derived from that
study, are licensed under CC BY-NC 4.0, and are NOT covered by the MIT
licence of qesR:

  - inst/extdata/dict/variables.csv.gz: variable names and labels of the
    data file, and question text in English and French quoted from the
    codebook (column doc_ref names the codebook entry of each text);
  - inst/extdata/dict/values.csv.gz: value labels of the data file
    (English), their French wording from the codebook, the missing-value
    type of each code, and the unweighted count of each code;
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/crosswalk.csv: the question wording quoted from
    the codebook (wording_en, wording_fr);
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/valuemaps.csv: the value labels of the data file
    (source_label);
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/gates.csv: unweighted counts of each answer to a
    question and to the question that filters it;
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/expected/marginals.csv: unweighted counts of
    each harmonized category;
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/expected/hashes.csv: md5 hashes of the
    harmonized columns, with the number of rows of the file;
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/relaxed_maps.csv: the question wording quoted
    from the codebook (wording_en, wording_fr) and unweighted counts of each
    code (evidence);
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/expected/relaxed_marginals.csv: unweighted counts
    of each relaxed category;
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/expected/relaxed_hashes.csv: md5 hashes of the
    relaxed columns, with the number of respondents or rows;
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/waves.csv and inst/extdata/harmonize/weights.csv:
    the size of each wave, its fieldwork dates and mode, and the weighting
    method, as the codebook publishes them (pp. 5 and 7);
  - inst/extdata/catalog/studies.csv: the notes of the qes2022 row
    (notes_en, notes_fr), which give the size of its two waves from the
    codebook's methodological note;
  - tests/testthat/fixtures/v044-get-qes-names.csv: the number of missing
    values of each column that qesR 0.4.4 returned for the study;
  - tests/testthat/fixtures/spec_legacy/valuemaps.csv: md5 hashes of value
    labels of the data file;
  - the harmonization reference, vignettes/harmonization-reference.Rmd and
    vignettes/fr-reference-harmonisation.Rmd (doc/harmonization-reference.html
    and doc/fr-reference-harmonisation.html of the installed package), which
    print the wording and value labels of crosswalk.csv and valuemaps.csv;
  - NEWS.md and the other vignettes where they quote labels or counts of
    qes2022: the search examples of get-started and fr-demarrage print
    variable labels of the study, and migrating-0.7 and fr-migrer-0.7 give
    the number of cells whose accents qesR repairs in its file;
  - the tests of the package (the R files of tests/testthat/) where they
    quote a variable label, question or value label of qes2022, or a count
    of its file, to check what qesR returns for it.

Attribution, as the licence requires. Source: Mahéo, Valérie-Anne;
Bélanger, Éric; Stephenson, Laura B; Harell, Allison, 2023, "2022 Quebec
Election Study", https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/PAQBDR, Harvard Dataverse,
V1.1 (qes_cite("qes2022") gives the full citation). Licensed under CC BY-NC
4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/). Changes made by
qesR: the labels and texts were extracted from the data file and the
codebook, whitespace squished and lines joined, the question of an item of
a grid written as its stem followed by the item in brackets, typed as
