The licence allows these files to be copied and adapted for non-commercial
purposes only, with the attribution above. When you use the study's data,
the same licence applies (qes_cite("qes2022"), whose text citation ends
with the licence and its URL). qesR repeats this notice, which says that
the material was adapted and points to this file for the changes and the
list of files, where it shows the study's metadata: a printed codebook of
qes2022 (print(qes_codebook("qes2022"))) and printed qes_search() results
that include it; the attribute "licence_notice" of its codebooks and of the
qes_question() and qes_search() results that include it, so that a saved
copy keeps it; and the harmonization reference
(vignettes/harmonization-reference.Rmd and fr-reference-harmonisation.Rmd,
built as doc/harmonization-reference.html and
doc/fr-reference-harmonisation.html of the installed package, which quote
the wording and labels of the spec, qes2022's included). The rest of qesR,
including the other rows of these files, stays under the MIT licence. The
files of qesR that only name the study, its variables or its codes (the
render rules of inst/extdata/harmonize/legacy.csv, targets.csv,
CHANGES.csv and inst/extdata/legacy/changes.csv, which hold no label,
wording or count) are qesR's own and are MIT. The rest of the catalog
entry of the study (title, authors, version, file names, sizes and
checksums, inst/extdata/catalog/) is the deposit's published metadata.

The development repository of qesR (not the package) also holds the rows of
qes2022 in inst/extdata/validation/validation_report.csv (section 3), under
the same licence and attribution, and the curated tables it builds the
dictionary from (data-raw/questions/qes2022.csv,
data-raw/questions/qes2022_values.csv and the qes2022 rows of
data-raw/questions/missing_labels.csv and missing_codes.csv).


3. Other sources
----------------

  - inst/extdata/catalog/elections.csv: election dates, from the official
    results published by Élections Québec (each row gives its source URL).
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/legacy.csv: how each column of get_qes_master()
    and get_decon() is rendered from the harmonized targets; and
    inst/extdata/legacy/: the columns qesR 0.4.4 stacked by name and no
    longer built (removed.csv), and the studies whose legacy values differ
    from qesR 0.4.4 (changes.csv, no counts); part of qesR (MIT).
  - Not in the package: inst/extdata/validation/official_results.csv and
    official_turnout.csv of the qesR source repository hold province-wide
    votes by party, valid and rejected ballots, ballots cast and registered
    electors of the Quebec general elections of 1998 to 2022, from the
    official results published by Élections Québec (its archived results
    files; each row gives its source URL). Parties other than those the
    harmonization spec names are summed into one row. Source: Élections
    Québec, official results of the general elections, © Directeur général
    des élections du Québec. Two sets of terms apply (consulted
    2026-09-27):
      (a) 2014, 2018 and 2022. Their results files are listed on dgeq.org,
      Élections Québec's open-data site, and are covered by its open-data
      licence, the "Licence d'utilisation des données ouvertes du
      directeur général des élections" (https://www.dgeq.org/licence.html;
      in English, https://www.dgeq.org/en/licence.html): free,
      non-exclusive and non-transferable, for any lawful purpose, commercial
      use included, with the rights to reproduce, translate, compile,
      communicate to the public and sub-license. It requires this notice at
      all times: « Comprend des données ouvertes octroyées sous la licence
      d'utilisation des données ouvertes du directeur général des
      élections disponible à l'adresse Web dgeq.org. L'octroi de la
      licence n'implique aucune approbation par le directeur général des
      élections de l'utilisation des données ouvertes qui en est faite. »
      ("Contains open data granted under the Chief Electoral Officer's Open
      Data User Licence available at http://dgeq.org/en/. The fact of
      granting the licence in no way implies that the Chief Electoral
      Officer approves the use made of the open data.") The user must also
      indemnify the Chief Electoral Officer, and the Chief Electoral
      Officer may cancel the licence without giving a reason, which cancels
      every sub-licence. It is not a Creative Commons or open-source
      licence.
      (b) 1998, 2007, 2008 and 2012. Their results files are not listed on
      dgeq.org (for 2012 it lists only the poll-level results and the
      candidate lists), so, on a cautious reading of the licence, which
      covers the data "available or referenced" on dgeq.org, they fall under
      Élections Québec's terms of use for its site
      (https://www.electionsquebec.qc.ca/en/our-institution/terms-of-use/),
      which allow reproduction for non-profit purposes with mention of the
      source and copyright and need its written permission for other uses
